{
  "0": "Across all four datasets, jointly set hidden/embedding width=192 and learning rate=0.0007, retaining one GRU layer, dropout=0.1, batch size=128 and maxlen=128. Greater recurrent capacity may distinguish diverse purchase sequences, while smaller updates stabilize the wider tied, unit-normalized representation. Preserve embedding parameterization, readout, scoring and loss; propagate dimensions through metadata and predictor loading.",
  "1": "Across all four datasets, replace final-state readout with causal exponential pooling of nonpadding GRU states using weights proportional to 0.8^(t-j) for j<=t, normalized over real positions. Apply the same pooling at every supervised training position and at the final real inference position. This may retain recent complementary interests while reducing dependence on the latest interaction. Preserve history truncation, embeddings, GRU topology, scoring, loss and optimizer; update the predictor writer consistently.",
  "2": "Across all four datasets, retain the existing pairwise-plus-0.2-pointwise loss but weight each nonpadding target by 0.95^(number of later nonpadding targets), dividing the weighted sum by the sum of weights. Emphasizing recent prediction positions may align training with next-item evaluation while retaining supervision from earlier prefixes. Keep sampling, embeddings, GRU topology, readout, scoring, optimizer and training settings fixed.",
  "3": "Across all four datasets, change the tied item embedding output from unit norm to fixed norm 0.75, preserving exact zero padding and width=128. Smaller input magnitudes may reduce GRU gate saturation, while smaller candidate magnitudes may moderate score growth under the mixed ranking/logistic objective. Test this embedding-only change independently of the earlier multi-change timeout. Preserve features, GRU topology, history policy, readout, scoring formula, loss and optimizer; mirror normalization in the predictor writer."
}
